{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["צֹר — like ״חַרְבוֹת צֻרִים״: a polished razor, a sharp blade. And likewise ״אַף תָּשִׁיב צוּר חַרְבּוֹ״ — the point of his sword was bent and warped. And therefore he did not engage him in combat." "And she cut, etc. — the commandment benefited Moshe to save him like an offering, in the manner that Gideon and Manoach did when the angel appeared to them." "וַתַּגַּע — a hifil form, but וַיִּגַּע in the four corners of the house is a qal form. ״To his feet״ — to Moshe's feet; the angel was appeased by this. For 'to the feet of the angel' he does not wish to explain, for who knows whether she saw the feet of the angel." "כִּי חֲתַן דָּמִים אַתָּה לִי — by these bloods my bridegroom remains mine." "חֲתָנִי — my husband."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashbam, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {0 "The lemma צֹר refers to the flint/zipporah used for circumcision in Exodus 4:25; Rashbam explains it as a sharp polished blade, and the verse from Psalms (Ps 89:44) supports reading צור as the blade of a sword.", 1 "The lemma ותכרות (וגו') is abbreviated; Rashbam reads the act of circumcision as functionally analogous to the sacrifices offered by Gideon (Judg 6) and Manoach (Judg 13) when visited by the angel.", 2 "Rashbam analyzes ותגע as hifil (causative) against ויגע as qal; he resists specifying whose feet were touched, declining the reading 'feet of the angel.'", 3 "Rashbam reads the phrase as expressing that through the blood of circumcision Moshe remains bound to her as husband (חתן = בעל, see next comment)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:40:30.925598757Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}